 triple Qs by splitting them.\n\nAlternative: start with triple ♠4♥4♣4 + attached pair, e.g., ♠10♣10. That uses 5 cards, leaving 15 — slower.\n\nOr play triple Qs with attached pair (e.g., ♥A♣A). Also 5 cards.\n\nBut the 11-card straight burns over half my hand instantly — huge tempo advantage. Peasants will likely need multiple turns to recover control, and I’ll be close to going out.\n\nHowever, risk: if a peasant holds Red Joker + rocket material or bombs, they might interrupt later with a bomb or rocket. But no bomb has been played yet, and I hold two 2s and BlackJoker — I can still defend if needed.\n\nAlso, by playing the long straight now, I avoid getting stuck later with too many mid-cards when peasants might force me to play inefficiently.\n\nAnother option: start small to probe, like pair ♠4♣4 — but that risks giving control early and letting peasants team up efficiently.\n\nBest move: play the maximum-length straight to minimize remaining cards and pressure opponents.\n\nThe longest valid straight in the list is:  \n**♠4 ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9 ♣10 ♠J ♣Q ♥K ♣A**\n\nIt uses exactly the cards I have and is listed in Valid Moves.\n\nReason: This move clears 11 cards in one turn, drastically reducing hand size and maximizing pressure on both peasants. It leverages my strong sequential cards before they can be broken up by opponent plays. Saving high singles and pairs (like 2s and jokers) for defense later is wise. This aggressive start exploits the landlord’s first-move advantage.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♠5", "♠6", "♦7", "♠8", "♠9", "♣10", "♠J", "♣Q", "♥K", "♣A"]}'}
